 that it is the  people that actually rules so that's my  main concern  if we are committed Democrats what would  the people have to be in order for  democracy to make sense okay and what  led you to pursue this well I'm a  political theorist so you could say that  simply trying to make sense of concepts  within a theory was enough but I also  believe that theories have to be in a  given context and they're only important  to the extent that they are in that  political historical context that we  care about so it is the political  context that I lived through that led me  to this question it's they overlap of my  interesting theory and two political  events that took place in the last  decade that were very very important for  me the first is they're American 2000  election and the second one is the 2006  election in Mexico in the first event I  had just arrived in in the United States  as a PhD student and I remember going to  a bar and watching the election returns  with my friends and I remember we all  said okay let's go to bed because  Florida had been cold and the gore was  being elected so we just went to bed and  I remember waking up the next day and it  hadn't been solved and as you remember  that went on for months months and my  main concern there is how can we say who  is making that decision who is the  people that is eventually going to  decide who's going to be the governor of  this country for or like the president  of this country for them in the next  four years and knowing who gets to  decide was something that really got me  going in trying to figure out who what  is the people and whether I can decide  and this of course  led me to the second event that it's  very interesting but it's also  interesting for personal reasons because  I am a Mexican and I lived through the  process of turning in Mexico from an  authoritarian regime into a democracy  and in this process one could say  culminated in the 2006 election when  something very similar happened in  Mexico you had an election that was too  close to call but unlike the process in  the United States where it ended with a  decision of the Supreme Court in Mexico  there one of the candidates just did not  concede the election him he pointed out  that the procedure had not been the way  it should have been and basically  thought that the legal institutions were  not legitimate and claimed that the  legitimacy of government had to come  back to a popular decision mm-hm and so  he went he brought his movement to the  streets and I was there at some moments  in which people claimed he mobilized up  to two million people on the street and  it was very interesting to see all that  enthusiasm and all that political the  strength of political action but at the  same time I was very skeptical of claims  saying that it is the people who are  making the decision okay and it was that  contrast that got me going and led me to  write the book what did happen in Mexico  well yeah eventually the candidate never  conceded but in the turn of events were  such that in the institution's just got  going and the decision of the newly  elected President Felipe Calderon was  taken as the factor of decision as a  matter of fact he was the president even  though Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador never  considered the election  interesting okay let's talk about your  concept of the people and how it is  different from current thinking well my  concept of the people is that the people  is a prize  so it's not a collection of individuals  it's it's a process that unfolds in time  this is quite different from all current  views so let me just tell you about the  others and why is it different um I  think that when you think about the  people most individuals in the street  the most common view is that the people  is something like a nation is perhaps a  people right so you can say these are  the songs of my people or something of  that kind  um for example in the Bible the most  sisters the 