 up the Institute  exactly institutions wouldn't exist  without the people and the other way  around the people wouldn't exist without  the institution's because it's a process  that makes one and the other and over  time so you can't simply let go of the  institution's however and this is the  second implication if the people can  judge institutions so you can have this  stance that allows you to say well are  these institutions legitimate or not and  this is exactly what's going on in  Tahrir Square throughout last year and  all throughout there are countries the  people are in fact saying what is the  legitimacy of these institutions but you  cannot simply ignore them you have it's  they're over the play back and forth  over time that would allow you to say  what is the legitimacy of these  institutions and the third one which I  can see there is the most important is  that if the people is a process  unfolding over time you cannot claim  that the people have said something the  people are always speaking so you can  say that there are certain decisions  that are influenced by the interplay of  ideas that are still going on but you  can all claim the people to yourself  that is a  this theoretical a account allows you to  say why it is wrong to say I represent  the ultimate decision of the people and  a given time so I think that this is  something that we can see every time  that there is a popular mobilization as  it happened last year in the Middle East  and whenever you have claims of a leader  to speak for the people I say very  interesting thank you so much for being  here with us today and sharing some of  your work you're welcome for more  information about professor or Choa and  her work please visit our website at  Yale ddu backslash Macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible by the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Centre for International and  area studies at Yale  you

